  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 133 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nHeading into Friday night, an upper level disturbance will be moving \ninto the region from northern Mexico. Rain coverage will be highest \nalong and south of I-10, but rain will be possible nearly areawide \nthrough Saturday. Amounts will vary greatly, but guidance is \nbeginning to come into consensus that amounts will top out around a \nhalf inch for areas around the Marfa Plateau as well as south \ntowards the Presidio Valley. Elsewhere, total amounts through \nSaturday end up between a quarter to a third of an inch. \nLocalized flash flooding remains possible, particularly in the \nhigh terrain areas across the Davis Mountains, Chinatis, and \nportions of Big Bend. Drier conditions prevail for the region from\nSunday onward. \n\nA cold front moves through the area late Sunday and this will bring \nafternoon highs down from the 60s into the 50s for most. These \ntemperatures will be well below normal, but West Texas and southeast \nNew Mexico will see very fall-like conditions through the middle of \nthe coming week. Overnight lows settle into the 40s to low 50s for \nmuch of the area. Another system approaches during this time, but \nlong range guidance remains uncertain about its track and subsequent \ninfluence on conditions for the region for late next week. Stay \ntuned!\n\n-Stickney",
